####################################################################
# cat2a_process_priority.rq — CAT2a condition. HAND-AUTHORED.
#
# Answers CQ9 (manuscript Table 1): which alarms coincide with other alarms that
# likely already convey equivalent clinical or technical information?
#
# NL rule (CAT2, "simultaneous alarms likely carrying overlapping or
# redundant information"): "If an active alarm of equal or higher priority
# reports on the same physiological property as an incoming alarm, then
# the incoming alarm can be silenced." (Revised 2026-11-25 from "represents
# the same biological process": on the p99 knowledge base a process groups
# properties that carry different information — heart rate and ST segment,
# PEEP and respiratory rate — so process level silenced 1,282 blueprint
# pairs, 969 of them across unrelated metrics.)
#
# Clause by clause:
#   "an incoming alarm"        — a PHYSIOLOGICAL alarm, at its arrival.
#                                Technical alarms that carry a metric
#                                report device state and are never
#                                silenced by CAT2a. Not one CAT1 has just
#                                flagged likely false positive (see
#                                FLAGGED ALARMS below).
#   "an active alarm"          — a physiological alarm of the same patient
#                                whose transient graph is in the store now,
#                                other than the incoming one, and not
#                                flagged likely false positive.
#   "equal or higher priority" — priorityRank(active) >= priorityRank
#                                (incoming), priority_rank.ttl. (The former
#                                Unknown-priority guards are gone: no p99
#                                blueprint has priority Unknown, and an
#                                alarm without a priority matches no
#                                blueprint.)
#   "the same physiological    — each metric's type, or a superclass of it,
#    property"                   carries inference.ttl's class-level
#                                restriction mda:approximates <property>,
#                                and the two properties are the SAME. A
#                                property is the finest level the ontology
#                                states what a metric measures at: variants
#                                of one property match (ABP systolic,
#                                diastolic and mean; SVR and SVRI; PEEP and
#                                peak pressure; minute and tidal volume),
#                                different properties never do, not even
#                                within one process. Deliberately NO curated
#                                "redundant with" relation between
#                                properties: no such pairing holds in every
#                                case (electrical heart rate and pulse rate
#                                differ exactly when one looks for
#                                atrioventricular dissociation). A metric
#                                without an approximates axiom never
#                                matches.
#   "overlapping or redundant" — (from the category header) the incoming
#                                alarm deviates in the SAME direction as the
#                                active one and is NOT MORE SEVERE: the
#                                active state is the incoming one or a
#                                refinement of it — mda:refinesConcept
#                                (hierarchy.ttl, section A) and the
#                                cross-scheme skos:broadMatch from the rhythm
#                                Absent to SeverelyDecreased (section C). A
#                                reversal (tachycardia -> bradycardia) or an
#                                escalation (bradycardia -> severe
#                                bradycardia) is new information. Rhythms
#                                follow the same hierarchy: an active VT
#                                covers an incoming "VF or VT" or "abnormal
#                                rhythm", never the reverse.
#   "can be silenced"          — for as long as an alarm that justified it
#                                at arrival is still active: the silence is
#                                stored (mdapoc:silencedBy, one link per
#                                justifying alarm, in the incoming alarm's
#                                transient graph) and LIFTED when the last of
#                                them ends while the incoming alarm is still
#                                active (cat2_lift.rq).
#
# FLAGGED ALARMS (agreed 2026-11-25, all rules): an alarm flagged likely
# false positive (CAT1) takes part in no other rule. As the incoming alarm
# it is not also silenced as redundant: "likely false" already decides it,
# and a second verdict would count it twice. As an active alarm it
# justifies no silence: redundancy with an alarm judged false would hide
# the one alarm that may be real (a valid second oximeter's desaturation
# silenced by a flagged first one). Both flags sit in the flagged alarm's
# own transient graph; CAT1 runs before CAT2 at every arrival, so the
# incoming alarm's flag is already stored, and an active alarm's status is
# settled (a CAT1b withdrawal removes its flag, after which it counts for
# later arrivals).
#
# GRAPHS (contract: structure persists, states are transient). ?gT is the
# incoming alarm's transient graph, ?gA an active alarm's: each holds the
# alarm's message and the STATE it reports on its metric (hasValueState or
# hasRhythm). A state found there is that alarm's report. The metric's
# TYPE is structure, read from the same alarm's own persistent graph (?gP,
# ?gAP), so no hop is borrowed from another alarm. The metric node is shared
# by every alarm on that pathway and may carry several states at once; the
# state inside an alarm's own transient graph is the one that alarm
# reported (no alarm ever edits another's graph). Priority rank, the
# approximates axioms and the state hierarchy are static facts in the
# default graph.
#
# CONTRACT. Bound on entry: ?alarm (the incoming alarm), ?now, ?incomingPrio
# (its priority: its own hasPriority triple is inserted only after its
# checks, so it can never justify its own silence). Binds: ?active (one row
# per active alarm justifying the silence; all are stored) and ?witness
# (that alarm's transient graph).
# Used by actions/check.rq and actions/silence_insert.rq.
#
# VALIDATION — engine/regression.py: cat2a_pos (severe bradycardia, then
# bradycardia), cat2a_gate (low MAP on two monitors) and cat2a_peak (PEEP
# high, then peak pressure high — same property RespirationPressure)
# silence; cat2a_neg, cat2a_opposite, cat2a_escalation, cat2a_reversal,
# cat2a_hr_map and cat2a_technical do not; cat2a_lift and cat2a_lift_last
# are silenced and lifted when the last justifying alarm ends;
# cat2a_flagged_justifier (a flagged active alarm justifies nothing) and
# cat2a_flagged_incoming (a flagged incoming alarm is not also silenced).
####################################################################

PREFIX mda:       <https://w3id.org/mda/ontology#>
PREFIX mdapoc:    <https://w3id.org/mda/poc#>
PREFIX alarmcat:  <https://w3id.org/mda/vocab/alarm-category/>
PREFIX rdf:       <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#>
PREFIX rdfs:      <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#>
PREFIX owl:       <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#>
PREFIX skos:      <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#>

# the incoming alarm: its report (transient) and its metric's type (persistent)
GRAPH ?gT { ?alarm mda:hasCategory alarmcat:Physiological .
            ?alarm mda:hasMessage ?msg . ?msg mda:concernsPatient ?patient .
            ?metric ?stateProp ?state . }
VALUES ?stateProp { mda:hasValueState mda:hasRhythm }
BIND(IRI(CONCAT(STR(?alarm), "#persistent")) AS ?gP)
GRAPH ?gP { ?metric rdf:type ?metricType . }
?metricType rdfs:subClassOf* ?r . ?r owl:onProperty mda:approximates ; owl:hasValue ?property .
?incomingPrio mda:priorityRank ?incomingRank .
# not flagged likely false positive (CAT1 has just run on it)
FILTER NOT EXISTS { GRAPH ?gT { ?alarm mdapoc:flaggedLikelyFalsePositive ?incomingFlagEvidence } }

# an active alarm of the same patient, reporting on the same property
GRAPH ?gA { ?active mda:hasCategory alarmcat:Physiological .
            ?active mda:hasMessage ?activeMsg . ?activeMsg mda:concernsPatient ?patient .
            ?active mda:hasPriority ?activePrio .
            ?activeMetric ?activeStateProp ?activeState . }
VALUES ?activeStateProp { mda:hasValueState mda:hasRhythm }
BIND(IRI(CONCAT(STR(?active), "#persistent")) AS ?gAP)
GRAPH ?gAP { ?activeMetric rdf:type ?activeMetricType . }
?activeMetricType rdfs:subClassOf* ?activeR . ?activeR owl:onProperty mda:approximates ; owl:hasValue ?property .
?activePrio mda:priorityRank ?activeRank .
# not flagged likely false positive
FILTER NOT EXISTS { GRAPH ?gA { ?active mdapoc:flaggedLikelyFalsePositive ?activeFlagEvidence } }
# the same direction, not more severe
?activeState (mda:refinesConcept|skos:broadMatch)* ?state .

FILTER(?active != ?alarm)
FILTER(?activeRank >= ?incomingRank)
BIND(?gA AS ?witness)
